WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.655
Many threat hunts begin with a hypothesis: a structured, testable assumption that guides the investigation.

2
00:00:06.655 --> 00:00:14.837
A useful hypothesis explains what the team is concerned about, why the concern is reasonable, and what evidence would help evaluate it.

3
00:00:14.837 --> 00:00:26.301
Hypotheses may come from threat intelligence, incident lessons, environmental changes, detection gaps, risk assessments, unusual trends, or questions raised by system owners.

4
00:00:26.301 --> 00:00:29.345
The source should connect the hunt to an organizational need.

5
00:00:29.345 --> 00:00:32.717
A well-formed hypothesis is narrow enough to investigate.

6
00:00:32.717 --> 00:00:41.777
It identifies the relevant environment, expected behavior, time period, and data sources without assuming that malicious activity has already occurred.

7
00:00:41.777 --> 00:00:46.796
Before analysis begins, hunters should define scope and success criteria.

8
00:00:46.796 --> 00:00:56.993
They should know which systems are included, which data is available, what limitations exist, and what observations would support, reject, or refine the hypothesis.

9
00:00:56.993 --> 00:01:02.411
Baseline knowledge is important because unusual does not automatically mean malicious.

10
00:01:02.411 --> 00:01:12.987
Maintenance, software deployment, administrative work, business travel, automation, and emergency operations can all create uncommon but legitimate patterns.

11
00:01:12.987 --> 00:01:16.390
As evidence develops, the hypothesis may change.

12
00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:24.112
A disciplined hunter follows the data, records alternative explanations, and avoids forcing observations into the original theory.

13
00:01:24.112 --> 00:01:28.553
This reduces confirmation bias and improves analytical credibility.

14
00:01:28.553 --> 00:01:40.795
The hunt record should capture the question, rationale, scope, queries or analytical methods at an appropriate level, evidence reviewed, limitations, conclusion, and recommended follow-up.

15
00:01:40.795 --> 00:01:43.928
Documentation makes the work repeatable and teachable.

16
00:01:43.928 --> 00:01:51.980
A conclusion may identify suspicious activity, reject the concern within the available evidence, or remain inconclusive.

17
00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:59.004
Each outcome can still reveal useful improvements to data, detections, processes, and future hunting questions.
